  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-7-PPR-4000003284</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-7" type="10">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the Netherlands</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SNV &#x2013; Value Chain and Youth Development in Cabo Delgado</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since August 2019, EKN has been funding the Value Chain and Youth Development programme in Cabo Delgado  province under the management of SNV. priginally the programme combined three projects: (a) Opportunities  for  Youth Employment (OYE), (b)  Soy value chain development,  and (c) Mango  value chain development. SNV implements the OYE project and the nutrition component that has beenIintegrated, while Technoserve (TNS) is in charge of the soy value chain project. The Aga Khan Foundation (AKF)  would implement the mango value chain in Macomia  district. A condition of the grant was to undertake an independent security  assessment of Macomia and surrounding districts to determine the risk associated with the mango intervention, in view of the growing insurgency in Cabo Delgado. The outcome of this assessment revealed that Macomia and neighboring districts were of high risk for the mango value chain project. This risk had been anticipated in the original BEMO. Subsequently, SNV has requested the replacement of the mango value chain by a horticulture project to be implemented  in Montepuez, Salama  and  amuno, districts not targeted by the insurgency. The project will be implemented  by SNV. Besides the expertise and experience, SNV is better positioned than Aga Khan to attract Dutch seed companies to invest in Mozambique. Also, the soy project should have been implemented through two stakeholder groups trained by TNS - Plexus, an agribusiness company  based in Montepuez  and Small Commercial  Farmers (SCFs)  - as means for transferring  technologies to Smallholder Farmers (SHFs). TNS had to adjust this strategy as Plexus, with whom TNS had agreed to engage on soy production  was no longer able to participate in the short term, due to financial difficulties post cyclone Kenneth (at least for this season). To cover this gap, TNS will hire 3 new field technicians  who will be assisted by 47 field promoters. In  addition, TNS has suggested to introduce a new component -soy processing  activities with women associations - to be initially piloted and then scaled-up. It seeks to  get communities  to start consuming  soy milk and soy flour providing both an economic opportunity  for rural women, as well as a basis for nutritious foods. The programme will implemented  by the 2 remaining partners, SNV (OYE and the horticulture  project) and TNS (soy value chain). The total programme&apos;s budget remains unchanged. There is a slight reduction in the programme management &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; co-ordination budget line to reinforce the nutrition component and add a new component regarding the soy processing for  human consumption. In addition, the soy budget will be increased to address the gap created by the discontinuity of Plexus.</narrative>
  </description>
